  "models_note": "The ONE model record table (0.22.0). A row is keyed by (provider, id) and carries provider, id, aliases, tier (free vocabulary: fast, balanced, deep today; a new tier is a new word here), in_per_mtok, out_per_mtok (null = not recorded: the ledger's flat output weight applies), cache_read (multiple of in_per_mtok, null = the flat 0.1), window ({tokens, verified}), efforts (list the model accepts; [] = none; absent = gt does not know), long_prompt ({over_tokens, multiple, verified} or null), verified (date the row was read from the provider's page; null = carried from the pre-0.22.0 tables, which kept no date), known (true on every row; an id that matches no row is known: false) and optional auto_clear_priors (the measured priors for gt_auto_clear.py: a model with none is skipped as 'not measured'). Numbers are exactly the pre-0.22.0 `prices`, `cache_read`, `windows` and `long_prompt` rows, one row per needle; tests/test_model_records.py pins that every id priced before is priced the same. MATCHING (gt_model_records.py): normalise (lower case, drop a trailing [..] context suffix, drop one of the provider's route_prefixes), then 1) an EXACT id or declared alias, 2) the provider's prefix_rules in order, first match wins (a rule is [prefix, row id]; keep a specific prefix before a general one; a test fails when a rule is shadowed), never a substring. A bare family word (opus, sonnet, haiku) is its own exact row so its numbers stay what they were. The legacy sections above (prices, cache_read, windows, long_prompt, ladder) are still read by gt_expiry_guard.py, gt_compact.py and gt_cut_advisor.py; a test pins that they agree with these rows.",
  "tier_defaults_note": "The default model (an exact id or alias in models) a tier resolves to, cheapest first: the order is the calibration tier table, and the last tier is the default judge.",
